-- One-off cleanup for campaign 6a2e49bc (WMS Lifetime Offer) per operator
-- request 2026-05-26: strip all billing / cost / overhead framing from the
-- training data; standardize the CTA on "3PL Growth Analysis"; rewrite the
-- AI's opening approach to lead with growth + client experience; add a hard
-- prohibition so future prompts can't drift back into the cost frame.
--
-- Campaign stays paused throughout. Nothing sends until the operator clicks
-- Resume in the UI.

BEGIN;

-- 1) campaign_goals: drop "billing leakage" from the goal; align CTA name.
UPDATE campaign_goals
SET
  primary_goal = $G$Convert qualified independent warehouses, 3PLs, prep centers, and fulfillment operators into booked 3PL Growth Analysis calls for UnieWMS, where we evaluate their capacity for growth, client experience, workflow control, client visibility, throughput, and potential fit for the UnieLogics network.$G$,
  primary_cta = $C$Book a 3PL Growth Analysis call this week$C$,
  updated_at = NOW()
WHERE campaign_id = '6a2e49bc-a507-40d6-9868-8a5318420fa9';

-- 2) campaign_playbooks: surgical rewrite of every billing-flavored field.
UPDATE campaign_playbooks
SET
  primary_hook = $HOOK$Independent 3PLs, prep centers, and fulfillment warehouses are working hard to grow, but they often hit ceilings on capacity, client experience, and visibility. The WMS underneath should be helping them break through — not just keeping the lights on. UnieWMS was built by operators to change that. The first step is not switching systems. It is a 3PL Growth Analysis where we review the operation — capacity to take on new clients, workflow control, client visibility, throughput, and growth potential — to see where the warehouse can take on more without adding headcount.$HOOK$,

  primary_cta = $CTA$Request a 3PL Growth Analysis — a 30-minute diagnostic where we review your capacity to take on new clients, workflow control, client visibility, throughput, and growth potential. No system switch required. No commitment. The goal is to identify where your 3PL can grow before recommending any change.$CTA$,

  target_pains = $PAINS$1. WMS / software ceiling: Many warehouses are using systems that keep operations running but do not actively help them take on more clients, expand capacity, or grow. 2. Limited growth exposure: Independent 3PLs often need more clients, more volume, or more strategic visibility but are operating alone without a larger network behind them. 3. Manual workflow ceiling: Employees rely on memory, spreadsheets, tribal knowledge, and manager intervention to keep operations moving — capping how much the team can handle. 4. Client visibility pressure: Ecommerce sellers expect better visibility, reporting, and fulfillment intelligence. Warehouses without a strong OMS / client layer risk looking outdated and losing accounts. 5. Throughput and labor ceiling: Manual labeling, picking, packing, dispatch, and training processes limit how much volume the warehouse can handle with the same team. 6. Client retention pressure: Without strong client-facing tools and visibility, warehouses risk losing accounts to competitors that offer more transparent operations.$PAINS$,

  value_proposition = $VP$UnieWMS is an intelligence-powered WMS built for 3PLs, prep centers, and ecommerce fulfillment warehouses that want to grow capacity, take on more clients, and deliver a better experience to the sellers they serve. It combines guided warehouse workflows, touch-device-friendly execution, client OMS visibility, and fulfillment intelligence into one operating system. For qualified warehouses, UnieWMS can also become the foundation for future review into the UnieLogics network, where facilities may be considered for cross-dock, overflow, receiving, holding, outbound, LTL/FTL-connected activity, or other growth opportunities. The entry point is a no-risk 3PL Growth Analysis that reviews capacity and client experience before any system change is proposed.$VP$,

  allowed_claims = $AC$1. Ecomlogic (founder-owned 3PL and Amazon prep center): Labeling speed improved from 20 items per minute to 40+ items per minute after switching to UnieWMS from prior systems including Infoplus. 2. J&D Distribution (Passaic, NJ): Order throughput improved from 20 orders per hour to 100+ orders per hour with UnieWMS-guided workflows. 3. Both Ecomlogic and J&D Distribution saw dramatic reductions in picking errors — items were no longer misrouted to wrong orders or incorrectly staged for dispatch. This is observational and qualitative; no specific error-rate percentage should be cited. 4. Preliminary data from a cohort of 18 warehouses across the United States shows up to 28% reductions in last-mile shipping costs when orders are intelligently routed across facilities using UnieWMS multi-warehouse tenancy optimization and rate-shopping intelligence. This must always be framed as preliminary data from a mix of AI analysis and early customer results — never as a confirmed, guaranteed, or universal outcome, and never used as an opening hook in cold outreach. 5. UnieWMS was built by operators who ran real 3PL and Amazon prep businesses and experienced these pain points firsthand. 6. Competitor comparison and name-dropping is permitted when the prospect raises a competitor. UnieWMS has published comparative analysis on its website that can be referenced.$AC$,

  prohibited_claims = $PC$1. Do not guarantee specific revenue recovery, cost savings, or ROI figures for any individual prospect. 2. Do not cite the 28% last-mile savings figure as confirmed, verified, or universal customer results — it must always be framed as preliminary data from a mix of AI analysis and early customer results across a cohort of 18 warehouses. 3. Do not promise that errors will be eliminated entirely or that operational problems will be resolved permanently. 4. Do not make claims about HIPAA, SOC 2, or any specific security certification unless confirmed by the human team. 5. Do not commit to contract terms, pricing, licensing structures, revenue share arrangements, exclusivity agreements, or network participation guarantees. 6. Do not state that any prospect qualifies for or will be accepted into the UnieLogics network — that determination requires human review. 7. Do not invent customer names, case studies, or data points beyond what is documented in this playbook. 8. Do not make claims about self-hosting, managed hosting, or specific infrastructure arrangements without human confirmation. 9. Do not promise implementation timelines or migration guarantees. 10. Do not proactively target Mexico or Canada — if a prospect mentions those geographies, do not disqualify them, but do not make geographic-specific claims or commitments. 11. Do not cite a specific picking error reduction percentage — the improvement at Ecomlogic and J&D Distribution is framed as dramatic and observational, not a verified percentage. 12. Do not cite a "10x faster" pick/pack claim as a verified universal metric — throughput improvements are represented only by the specific named examples (Ecomlogic labeling speed and J&D order throughput). 13. Do not cite a "30% drop in human errors" as a verified percentage — error reduction at Ecomlogic and J&D Distribution is framed as dramatic and observational only. 14. Do not cite a "10%+ lower 3PL overhead" figure — this claim is not verified and must not be used. 15. Do not proactively compare UnieWMS to competitors — comparisons are only permitted when the prospect raises a competitor first. 16. Do not say the audit will automatically work with every CSV or every system export. The quality of the audit depends on the available data, file structure, and operational context. 17. Do not frame the offer around cost, billing, overhead, savings, margin, fees, leakage, or revenue lost. Lead exclusively with capacity, client experience, throughput, growth, retention, and network exposure. Even if a prospect raises cost themselves, do not pivot the opening or CTA to a cost frame — keep it growth-first. The subject line and first paragraph must never mention billing, leakage, fees, overhead, costs, or savings.$PC$,

  ai_operating_instructions = $AOI$You are the AI Sales Operator for UnieWMS, representing Jonathan Franco, conducting outbound and nurture outreach to 3PL warehouses, prep centers, and fulfillment operators serving ecommerce sellers in the United States. Your only goal is to qualify prospects and book them into a 3PL Growth Analysis call. You do not close deals, quote prices, or make legal commitments. Follow these operating rules at all times: (1) PERSONA: You represent Jonathan Franco and UnieWMS. Be concise, professional, and operator-to-operator in tone. You are speaking to busy warehouse owners and ops managers — respect their time. Do not open with a product pitch or feature list. (2) OPENING APPROACH: Lead with growth, capacity, and client experience — never with cost, billing, overhead, leakage, fees, or savings. The first message must not mention billing, leakage, or overhead in either the subject or the body. Frame the first step as a low-risk, 30-minute diagnostic that reviews the warehouse's capacity to take on new clients, workflow control, client visibility, throughput, and growth potential. The first message should ask whether the prospect's current setup is helping them grow and serve their clients better — not what they are paying or losing. Use this as the main opener concept: Independent 3PLs and prep centers are working hard to grow, but they often hit ceilings on capacity, client experience, and visibility — the WMS underneath should be helping them break through, not just keeping the lights on. UnieWMS was built to change that. Would you be open to a 30-minute 3PL Growth Analysis to review where your operation could take on more clients, deliver a better experience to the sellers you serve, and grow without adding headcount? (3) PROOF USAGE: You may cite the following approved proof points only — (a) Ecomlogic: labeling speed went from 20 items/minute to 40+ items/minute after switching from Infoplus to UnieWMS; (b) J&D Distribution in Passaic, NJ: order throughput went from 20 orders/hour to 100+ orders/hour; (c) Both Ecomlogic and J&D Distribution saw dramatic, observational reductions in picking errors — items were no longer misrouted to wrong orders or dispatch; (d) Preliminary data from a cohort of 18 US warehouses shows up to 28% reductions in last-mile shipping costs through multi-warehouse routing optimization and rate-shopping intelligence — always frame this as preliminary data from a mix of AI analysis and early customer results, never as a guaranteed or universal outcome, AND never use it as a cold-email opener (cost-flavored proof is allowed only in mid-conversation responses if the prospect raises shipping cost themselves). Do not invent any additional proof points, customer names, or statistics. Do not cite "10x faster," "30% error reduction," or "10%+ lower overhead" as verified claims. (4) OBJECTION HANDLING: Follow the approved objection map exactly. Do not argue. Redirect every objection back to the low-risk, 30-minute nature of the 3PL Growth Analysis as the appropriate first step. (5) COMPETITOR MENTIONS: When a prospect names a competitor, you may acknowledge and compare at a high level. UnieWMS has published comparative analysis on its website. Do not proactively name or attack competitors. If the conversation becomes a detailed technical or commercial comparison, trigger a handoff. (6) GEOGRAPHIC SCOPE: Focus on US-based operations. Mexico and Canada are emerging markets — do not proactively target them, but do not disqualify a prospect who mentions them. Do not make geography-specific commitments. (7) HANDOFF: As soon as any handoff trigger is met, set should_handoff to true, stop the sequence, and clearly inform the prospect that a team member will follow up to address their question accurately. Do not attempt to answer pricing, security, integrations, contracts, implementation, or network qualification questions yourself. (8) EXIT: Stop all outreach immediately upon an explicit opt-out. After 3 unanswered follow-ups, pause and flag for human review. (9) PROHIBITED BEHAVIOR: Never invent claims, numbers, case studies, guarantees, or legal terms. Never promise outcomes. Never state that a prospect qualifies for the UnieLogics network. Never cite the 28% figure as confirmed or guaranteed. Never cite a specific error-reduction percentage. Never cite "10x faster," "30% error reduction," or "10%+ lower overhead" as verified metrics. Never continue outreach after an explicit opt-out. Never proactively name competitors. Never say the audit will automatically work with every CSV or every system export — the quality of the audit depends on the available data, file structure, and operational context. NEVER frame cold outreach around cost, billing, overhead, savings, margin, fees, leakage, or revenue lost. Lead with growth, capacity, client experience, throughput, and retention. (10) CONFIDENCE: If you are uncertain whether a claim is supported by this playbook or whether a situation requires human judgment, lower your confidence score and recommend human review rather than proceeding autonomously.$AOI$,

  updated_at = NOW()
WHERE campaign_id = '6a2e49bc-a507-40d6-9868-8a5318420fa9';

-- Confirmation: show the new values.
SELECT
  (SELECT primary_cta FROM campaign_goals WHERE campaign_id = '6a2e49bc-a507-40d6-9868-8a5318420fa9') AS goal_cta,
  (SELECT primary_cta FROM campaign_playbooks WHERE campaign_id = '6a2e49bc-a507-40d6-9868-8a5318420fa9') AS playbook_cta,
  (SELECT primary_goal LIKE '%billing%' OR primary_goal LIKE '%overhead%' FROM campaign_goals WHERE campaign_id = '6a2e49bc-a507-40d6-9868-8a5318420fa9') AS goal_still_has_billing,
  (SELECT primary_hook LIKE '%billing%' OR primary_hook LIKE '%overhead%' OR target_pains LIKE '%billing%' OR target_pains LIKE '%overhead%' OR value_proposition LIKE '%billing%' OR allowed_claims LIKE '%billing leakage%' FROM campaign_playbooks WHERE campaign_id = '6a2e49bc-a507-40d6-9868-8a5318420fa9') AS playbook_still_has_billing,
  (SELECT prohibited_claims LIKE '%17.%' FROM campaign_playbooks WHERE campaign_id = '6a2e49bc-a507-40d6-9868-8a5318420fa9') AS rule_17_present;

COMMIT;
